A demand for a valid type the only matching template cannot produce becomes an
injector input, not an error. Box has no constraint, so Box[int] is a valid type,
but the sole producer Make requires T: Stringer, so Make cannot build Box[int].
Per “unprovided means input”, Box[int] becomes a parameter (the user wires it in),
while Make still serves the valid consumer at Box[Name]. This is the deliberate
permissive behavior (never reject valid Go) and the point where plumb declines
to treat a constraint near-miss as a misuse.
-- config --
import-path: example.com/app
package-name: app
output: plumb_gen.go
-- example.com/app/a.go --
package app

type Stringer interface{ String() string }

type Name string

func (n Name) String() string { return string(n) }

type Box[T any] struct{ V T }

//plumb:build
func Make[T Stringer]() Box[T] { return Box[T]{} }

//plumb:build
func UseGood(b Box[Name]) int { return 0 }

//plumb:build
func UseInput(b Box[int]) string { return "" }
